SUMMARY:Worship (In person and Online)
DESCRIPTION:Order of Worship – Eleventh Sunday after Pentecost\nService for the Lord’s Day\nGATHERING AROUND THE WORD\n🎵 New! Hymns are now available on this page. Simply click any hymn title in the order of worship to view the music.🎵\nPRELUDE: Nocturne in C# minor\, op. posthumous; Chopin\n† CALL TO WORSHIP: Psalm 85:8-13\nGod speaks peace to the faithful\,\nto those who turn to God in their hearts.\nSurely salvation is at hand\nfor those who fear God.\nWhere God dwells\, steadfast love and faithfulness meet;\nrighteousness and peace kiss each other.\nFaithfulness springs up from the ground\nand righteousness looks down from the sky.\nGod gives what is good\nand we respond with abundant praise!\nCome\, let us worship God! \n† HYMN #159: “O Sing a Song of Bethlehem”\n† Prayer of Invocation\nCALL TO CONFESSION\nPRAYER OF CONFESSION\nGracious God\, you call us to step out in faith\, trusting in you for all things. We respond to your command but then sink in doubt and fear. We hide from the challenges of bold discipleship. Forgive us\, we pray\, when we confess with our lips but do not believe in our hearts. We know the way that leads to life\, but we get easily distracted by all the noise that prevents us from hearing you speak. Silence within us every voice but your own\, the voice we hear most clearly in silence: \nSilence. \nassurance of Pardon\nFrom the silent depths of God\,\nwe hear the word our souls long to hear\,\nthe word we heard through Jesus Christ:\nYou are forgiven.\nYou are loved.\nYou are mine.\nYou are free to begin again.\nReceive this good news and live from its peace.\nThanks be to God! \n† RESPONSE of Praise #582: “Glory to God Whose Goodness Shines on Me”\n† passing of the peace\nLIFE IN COMMUNITY\nPROCLAIMING THE WORD\nCHILDREN’S MESSAGE\nPRAYER FOR ILLUMINATION\nGracious God\, we listen for you in wind\, earthquake\, and fire. Unexpectedly\, you speak in the sound of silence. We pray that by the power of the Holy Spirit you will silence in us all the storms\, doubts\, and fears that overwhelm so that we may hear what you have to say. Your still small voice. Thin. Quiet. Yet compelling\, convicting\, commanding\, all the same. We ask it in the name of Christ. Amen. \nScripture: 1 Kings 19:9-18\n9 At that place he came to a cave and spent the night there. \nThen the word of the Lord came to him\, saying\, “What are you doing here\, Elijah?” 10 He answered\, “I have been very zealous for the Lord\, the God of hosts\, for the Israelites have forsaken your covenant\, thrown down your altars\, and killed your prophets with the sword. I alone am left\, and they are seeking my life\, to take it away.” \n11 He said\, “Go out and stand on the mountain before the Lord\, for the Lord is about to pass by.” Now there was a great wind\, so strong that it was splitting mountains and breaking rocks in pieces before the Lord\, but the Lord was not in the wind\, and after the wind an earthquake\, but the Lord was not in the earthquake\, 12 and after the earthquake a fire\, but the Lord was not in the fire\, and after the fire a sound of sheer silence. 13 When Elijah heard it\, he wrapped his face in his mantle and went out and stood at the entrance of the cave. Then there came a voice to him that said\, “What are you doing here\, Elijah?” 14 He answered\, “I have been very zealous for the Lord\, the God of hosts\, for the Israelites have forsaken your covenant\, thrown down your altars\, and killed your prophets with the sword. I alone am left\, and they are seeking my life\, to take it away.” 15 Then the Lord said to him\, “Go\, return on your way to the wilderness of Damascus; when you arrive\, you shall anoint Hazael as king over Aram. 16 Also you shall anoint Jehu son of Nimshi as king over Israel\, and you shall anoint Elisha son of Shaphat of Abel-meholah as prophet in your place. 17 Whoever escapes from the sword of Hazael\, Jehu shall kill\, and whoever escapes from the sword of Jehu\, Elisha shall kill. 18 Yet I will leave seven thousand in Israel\, all the knees that have not bowed to Baal\, and every mouth that has not kissed him.” \n† HYMN #169: “Dear Lord and Father of Mankind”\nScripture: Matthew 14:22-33\n22 Immediately he made the disciples get into a boat and go on ahead to the other side\, while he dismissed the crowds. 23 And after he had dismissed the crowds\, he went up the mountain by himself to pray. When evening came\, he was there alone\, 24 but by this time the boat\, battered by the waves\, was far from the land\,[a] for the wind was against them. 25 And early in the morning he came walking toward them on the sea. 26 But when the disciples saw him walking on the sea\, they were terrified\, saying\, “It is a ghost!” And they cried out in fear. 27 But immediately Jesus spoke to them and said\, “Take heart\, it is I; do not be afraid.” \n28 Peter answered him\, “Lord\, if it is you\, command me to come to you on the water.” 29 He said\, “Come.” So Peter got out of the boat\, started walking on the water\, and came toward Jesus. 30 But when he noticed the strong wind\,[b] he became frightened\, and\, beginning to sink\, he cried out\, “Lord\, save me!” 31 Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught him\, saying to him\, “You of little faith\, why did you doubt?” 32 When they got into the boat\, the wind ceased. 33 And those in the boat worshiped him\, saying\, “Truly you are the Son of God.” \nSermon: “The Silence is Not Empty;” Kenneth Kovacs\nRESPONDING TO THE WORD\nCALL TO OFFERING\nOFFERING OF TITHES AND GIFTS\nOFFERTORY: Love Medley; arr. Marlynn Ham\nO how He loves you and me\,\nO how He loves you and me;\nHe gave His life— what more could He give?\nO how He loves you;\nO how He loves me;\nO how He loves you and me! \nJesus to Calv’ry did go\,\nHis love for mankind to show;\nwhat He did there brought hope from despair;\nO how He loves you;\nO how He loves me;\nO how He loves you and me! \n† doxology\nPraise God\, from whom all blessings flow;\nPraise Christ\, all people here below;\nPraise Holy Spirit evermore;\nPraise Triune God\, whom we adore. Amen. \n† PRAYER OF DEDICATION\nGenerous God\, we thank you for your call and claim upon our lives. Because there is so much more we can become and so much more we can do\, we pray that our faith will increase\, that our practice of generosity will be enlarged; and that our joy in believing will encourage others as we share with them the good news of Jesus Christ; in whose name we pray. Amen. \nPrayers of the People and the Lord’s Prayer\nOur Father\, who art in heaven\, hallowed be thy name.\nThy kingdom come\, thy will be done\,\non earth as it is in heaven.\nGive us this day our daily bread;\nand forgive us our debts\, as we forgive our debtors;\nand lead us not into temptation but deliver us from evil.\nFor thine is the kingdom and the power\nand the glory\, forever. Amen. \n† HYMN #543: “God Be the Love to Search and Keep Me”\n† charge and benediction\nStep out in faith\,\ntrusting that the God who has called you is able to keep you from falling\nand hold you in love.\nAmen.\nMay the steadfast love and faithfulness of God surround you;\nthe peace of Christ enfold you;\nand the Holy Spirit encourage you now and forever.\nAmen. \nPOSTLUDE: Sweet Bye and Bye Variations; Walter C. Stier\nWorship Participants\nKathy Glennan\, Videographer\nNorman Lazarus\, Liturgist\nLum Offutt\, Pianist \nANNOUNCEMENTS\nBackpacks for Success\nMany students in our community lack access to school supplies. Backpacks\, pens\, pencils\, markers\, crayons\, and rulers are instrumental in preparing children for success. We’re calling on our members\, friends\, and Catonsville neighbors to support our local students through financial contributions and the purchase of new school supplies. All backpacks\, school supplies\, and financial contributions will be distributed to local schools. \nPlease drop supplies off in the vestibule between the Fellowship Hall and sanctuary between now and Wednesday\, August 19. \nYou can find the list of supplies as well as contribute financially by visiting catonsvillepres.org/news/backpack4success/.  \nThank you for supporting our local students and starting the year off with generosity! \nVolunteers Needed for Summer Food Collection\nVolunteers are needed to help collect food donations for the CEA Food Pantry every other Friday throughout the summer from 4:00–6:00 p.m. in the Frederick Road parking lot. The next collection is Friday\, August 14. \nVolunteers will greet donors\, collect food donations\, and help move the donated items to the storage room in Fellowship Hall. Please bring a lawn chair if you are able. To sign up\, visit https://tinyurl.com/CPCFoodDriveSignup or contact the church office for assistance. \nFall Messenger Submissions\nIt’s almost time for our fall edition of The Messenger. Send your submissions to Aisha at awhite@catonsvillepres.org by Monday\, August 17. Don’t miss your chance to be included! \nReconciliation: Overcoming Obstacles and Facilitating Resolutions | Tuesday\, August 25 | 7:00 p.m. in the library | Led by Dr. Ralph Surette\nHow can deep conflicts between us be resolved peacefully? Why can’t we discuss it? Psychological and emotional dynamics involved in both political and personal struggles can impede the reconciliation of differences. This presentation examines polarization\, anger\, blaming\, prejudice\, and forgiveness. We will explore how the process of reconciliation must include a cessation of mutual hostility\, both sides agreeing to learn more about their respective differences\, and a willingness to reach an accord while creating a mutual narrative that could provide a new vision of what each side aspires to realize and achieve. Join us as we explore practical approaches to overcoming conflict\, fostering understanding\, and building pathways toward reconciliation. \nPresbyterian Women Monday Morning Circle\nAll women are invited to join the Catonsville Presbyterian Church Monday Morning Presbyterian Women (PW) Circle for Bible study. This year’s national PW/Horizons Bible study explores Paul’s first letter to the Corinthians through eight to nine engaging lessons. \nThe Monday Morning Circle meets on Zoom on the first Monday of each month\, September through June. If the first Monday falls on a holiday\, the group meets on the second Monday instead. \nIf you are interested in participating in this year’s study\, please contact Brenda Logue at naylogue@aol.com or speak with another member of the circle. Study guides are $15 and will be ordered for the group in mid-August. All are welcome! \nCPC Trivia\nOn Friday\, September 18 at 7:00 p.m. in the library\, we will be celebrating the start of the program year with CPC Trivia! \nThere will be questions about the church\, the staff\, Catonsville\, and Presbyterianism in general. I fully expect everyone will come away having learned something new! \nCookies and lemonade will be provided. If you have any questions\, please e-mail Stephanie Stevens at shfemrite@gmail.com. RSVPs are not required! ~Stephanie \nCPC at the Catonsville Fall Festival\nDate:               Saturday\, September 26\, 2026\nTime:              10:00 a.m. – 4:00 p.m.\nLocation:       The Children’s Home\, 205 Bloomsbury Ave\, Catonsville\, MD 21228 \nCPC will once again have a booth at Catonsville Fall Festival. We will have activities for all ages in our booth. We are looking for volunteers to assist in engaging with the community and explaining these activities. Volunteers can sign up for any 1-hour time slot from 9:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.\, as we are looking for volunteers to be stationed in the booth throughout the entire event. Please sign up at https://tinyurl.com/CPCFallFest2026 or email Kevin Flis at kevinflis@gmail.com if you are interested in volunteering. For all others\, we invite you to come visit our booth and hope to see you at the Catonsville Fall Festival! \nFellowship Hour\nWhile Fellowship Hour continues through the summer\, it is at a much lower scale than it is throughout the year. Two years ago\, Dorothy Boulton herself described it as “So easy\, a pastor could do it!” All that is needed is a bag of pretzels (or something similar!) and a package of cookies. Water and lemonade are the only beverages — no coffee! Sign-up sheets will be on the table\, or you can email me at shfemrite@gmail.com to sign up. ~Stephanie \nAssociate Pastor Schedule\nDorothy Boulton will be on vacation until August 14. \nSummer Office Hours\nThe church office will be open Tuesday–Friday\, 9:00 a.m.–4:00 p.m. during August. \n\nSources \n“O Sing a Song of Bethlehem” Text: Louis FitzGerald Benson\, 1899. Music: English Country Songs\, 1893; harm. Ralph Vaughan Williams\, 1906. \n“Dear Lord and Father of Mankind” Text: John Greenleaf Whittier\, 1872. Music: Frederick Charles Maker\, 1887. \n“Glory to God\, Whose Goodness Shines on Me” Text: Trad. liturgical text; adapt. Paul M. Vasile\, 2008. Music: Paul M. Vasile\, 2008. Text Adapt. and Music © 2008 Paul M. Vasile. \n“God\, Be the Love to Search and Keep Me” Text and Music: Richard Bruxvoort Colligan\, 2004. Text and Music © 2004 This Here Music\, Worldmaking.net. \nPermission to podcast/stream the music in this service obtained from One License with license #A-734388; CCLI Copyright License B 20481088 + Streaming License B 48107.
